|* ro_array_core as rebuilt -- combining fidelity, frequency ladder, array current
|*
|* Purpose: this is the assembled-block measurement for issue #13's rebuild.
|* It drives the COMMITTED design/ro_array_core.spice -- the netlist
|* design/netlist.py exports from design/xschem/ro_array_core.sch and guards
|* with --check -- so what it measures is the schematic as drawn, N = 4
|* five-stage rings and all, not a hand-built approximation of it.
|*
|* Three things it establishes, none of which #10's campaign could:
|*
|*   1. COMBINING FIDELITY, in two separate senses that must not be
|*      conflated.
|*
|*      (a) EDGE RETENTION -- a bandwidth measure. The XOR tree's ceiling on
|*      N (sim/xor-combining-bandwidth/) is derived from a single gate driven
|*      by a periodic square wave. The real combining node is driven by the
|*      superposition of 4 skewed rings, whose inter-edge gaps are spread,
|*      not uniform: even when the MEAN gap is comfortably above the gate's
|*      minimum pulse width, the rings beat against each other and edge pairs
|*      pass through near-coincidence, where they are swallowed. This deck
|*      measures the realized rising-edge rate at a node and divides it by
|*      the ideal rate sum(1/T_i) over the rings feeding it, taken from the
|*      SAME run's per-ring periods.
|*
|*      Because the tree is BALANCED, one run gives that ratio at two array
|*      sizes for free: xdut.t1 combines 2 rings and xo combines all 4. No
|*      separate N-sweep deck is needed, and both numbers come from identical
|*      rings under identical conditions.
|*
|*      (b) VALUE FIDELITY -- a bias measure, and the one that actually
|*      reaches the sampler. A swallowed pulse is a swallowed PAIR of edges,
|*      and two missed toggles leave the node's parity unchanged, so the
|*      sampled value still tracks the ideal XOR of all four rings except
|*      inside the short swallowed window itself. Systematic bias at the
|*      combining node is what would destroy raw min-entropy, so this deck
|*      measures the time-average level of each combining node directly. A
|*      node whose mean sits at 0.5 x Vdd is combining without a DC error,
|*      however many edges it dropped.
|*
|*      Reporting only (a) would overstate the damage; reporting only (b)
|*      would hide that the tree is filtering at all. Both are recorded.
|*
|*   2. THE FREQUENCY LADDER, realized. ro_array_core.sch skews the rings by
|*      starve-device width, wstv = 0.42 um to 0.48 um in 0.02 um steps. What
|*      that ladder does to the actual oscillation frequencies is a device
|*      fact, not a design intent, and integer frequency ratios are the ones
|*      that mutually injection lock. This deck reports all four periods
|*      from one run so the realized ratios can be read off directly.
|*
|*   3. ARRAY SUPPLY CURRENT. Four separate vddr sources plus the block vdd,
|*      each measured separately, summed into a total. design/README.md's
|*      power rows had no sky130 measurement at all behind them.
|*
|* Start-up is by ENABLE RELEASE, not by .ic. A deterministic transient of a
|* ring started at its own DC operating point sits at the metastable point
|* and never oscillates, and the rings' internal nodes are inside a subckt
|* here (unlike #10's in-place ring decks, which could .ic them from the top
|* level). So all four enables are held at 0 through the DC operating point
|* and released together at t = 5 ns: with en = 0 the ro_nand2 pins its own
|* output high, which is a defined state, and releasing it starts the ring.
|* That is also how the block is actually meant to be brought up.
|*
|* Measurement window is 60 ns to 200 ns, i.e. 55 ns after release -- more
|* than ten periods of the slowest ring measured anywhere in the grid.
|*
|* Edge retention uses rising crossings 2 through 50 at xo rather than a
|* whole-vector transition count: it is a rate over 48 intervals, it needs no
|* vector-length arithmetic, and crossing 50 stays reachable inside the
|* window even if the tree were swallowing half the edges. Crossing 1 is
|* discarded as start-up.
|*
|* PVT: three (temp, supply) points, each bundling the tt/ss/ff process axis:
|*   -40 degC / 1.62 V   the entropy-binding corner (ss). Slowest rings, so
|*                       the widest edge spacing -- where retention should be
|*                       highest and where the entropy requirement binds.
|*    27 degC / 1.80 V   nominal
|*   -40 degC / 1.98 V   the fastest rings measured anywhere in #10's grid
|*                       (ff) -- the narrowest edge spacing, and also the
|*                       corner sim/ro-array-operating-point/ found BINDS the
|*                       combining bound. This is the hardest test of the
|*                       combining tree, and where retention is expected to
|*                       be WORST.
|*
|* This file is a TEMPLATE: sim/bin/corner-run.py substitutes /home/ubuntu/.volare/volare/sky130/versions/c6d73a35f524070e85faff4a6a9eef49553ebc2b/sky130A/libs.tech/combined/sky130.lib.spice,
|* ss, /home/ubuntu/GitHub/sky130-trng/.loom/worktrees/issue-13/design/ro_array_core.spice (pointed at design/ro_array_core.spice via
|* --ro-ring5), 27.0, 1.8 and 5p. As committed it is not a
|* runnable deck.
|
|.option scale=1u
|.lib "/home/ubuntu/.volare/volare/sky130/versions/c6d73a35f524070e85faff4a6a9eef49553ebc2b/sky130A/libs.tech/combined/sky130.lib.spice" ss
|
|.include "/home/ubuntu/GitHub/sky130-trng/.loom/worktrees/issue-13/design/ro_array_core.spice"
|
|.temp 27.0
|
|* per-ring supplies, one source per ring so each current is separable
|Vddr1 vddr1 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr2 vddr2 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr3 vddr3 0 dc 1.8
|Vddr4 vddr4 0 dc 1.8
|* block supply: combining tree + the four ring output buffers
|Vdd vdd 0 dc 1.8
|
|* enables: held low through the DC solution, released together at 5 ns
|Ven1 en1 0 PULSE(0 1.8 5n 100p 100p 1u 2u)
|Ven2 en2 0 PULSE(0 1.8 5n 100p 100p 1u 2u)
|Ven3 en3 0 PULSE(0 1.8 5n 100p 100p 1u 2u)
|Ven4 en4 0 PULSE(0 1.8 5n 100p 100p 1u 2u)
|
|xdut en1 en2 en3 en4 vddr1 vddr2 vddr3 vddr4 vdd 0 xo ro1 ro2 ro3 ro4 ro_array_core
|
|.tran 5p 200n
|
|.control
|run
|* --- per-ring periods, 10 whole periods each, first two edges discarded ---
|meas tran t1a when v(ro1)=0.5*1.8 rise=3
|meas tran t1b when v(ro1)=0.5*1.8 rise=13
|meas tran t2a when v(ro2)=0.5*1.8 rise=3
|meas tran t2b when v(ro2)=0.5*1.8 rise=13
|meas tran t3a when v(ro3)=0.5*1.8 rise=3
|meas tran t3b when v(ro3)=0.5*1.8 rise=13
|meas tran t4a when v(ro4)=0.5*1.8 rise=3
|meas tran t4b when v(ro4)=0.5*1.8 rise=13
|let tr1 = (t1b - t1a)/10
|let tr2 = (t2b - t2a)/10
|let tr3 = (t3b - t3a)/10
|let tr4 = (t4b - t4a)/10
|let f_ideal2 = 1/tr1 + 1/tr2
|let f_ideal = 1/tr1 + 1/tr2 + 1/tr3 + 1/tr4
|let skew_span = tr1/tr4
|* --- combining node: swing and realized rising-edge rate ---
|meas tran vxo_max MAX v(xo) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran vxo_min MIN v(xo) from=60n to=200n
|let swing_frac_xo = (vxo_max - vxo_min) / 1.8
|meas tran txo_a when v(xo)=0.5*1.8 rise=2
|meas tran txo_b when v(xo)=0.5*1.8 rise=50
|let f_xo = 48/(txo_b - txo_a)
|let edge_retention = f_xo / f_ideal
|* the same ratio one tree level down, i.e. at N = 2, from the same rings in
|* the same run (balanced tree, so t1 combines rings 1-2)
|meas tran tt1a when v(xdut.t1)=0.5*1.8 rise=2
|meas tran tt1b when v(xdut.t1)=0.5*1.8 rise=50
|let f_t1 = 48/(tt1b - tt1a)
|let retention_n2 = f_t1 / f_ideal2
|let retention_n4 = edge_retention
|* --- value fidelity: time-average level of each combining node ---
|meas tran vxo_avg AVG v(xo) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran vt1_avg AVG v(xdut.t1) from=60n to=200n
|let bias_xo = vxo_avg / 1.8
|let bias_t1 = vt1_avg / 1.8
|* --- supply currents (sign-flipped to read as consumed current) ---
|meas tran i1 AVG i(Vddr1) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran i2 AVG i(Vddr2) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran i3 AVG i(Vddr3) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran i4 AVG i(Vddr4) from=60n to=200n
|meas tran ib AVG i(Vdd) from=60n to=200n
|let i_rings = -(i1 + i2 + i3 + i4)
|let i_block = -ib
|let i_array_total = i_rings + i_block
|let p_array_total = i_array_total * 1.8
|print tr1
|print tr2
|print tr3
|print tr4
|print skew_span
|print f_ideal
|print f_xo
|print edge_retention
|print retention_n2
|print retention_n4
|print bias_t1
|print bias_xo
|print swing_frac_xo
|print i_rings
|print i_block
|print i_array_total
|print p_array_total
|.endc
|.end
|

